{"timestamp": "2026-08-11T15:40:37.146580+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-7c1102bc-3005-477b-9c05-8b4d54857208', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You design one complete lesson.\\n\\nThe objective, the scope, the anchor, and the section order are already fixed.\\nYou do not reopen them. Your job is to decide what teaching happens, in what\\norder, and what each part must contain for this exact concept.\\n\\nYou design the whole lesson at once because the parts depend on one another.\\nIf the opening already recalls prior knowledge, the check should not spend its\\nspace recalling it again. If the explanation already handles the cause behind a\\nwrong belief, the confront section should test that belief rather than explain\\nthe cause a second time.\\n\\nYou choose no formats. You will not see any.\\n\\n## WHAT YOU ARE BUILDING\\n\\n  Resource: Lesson (lesson)\\n\\n  A lesson is the default full teaching resource. It introduces a concept,\\n  builds understanding, models the method, gives students guided-to-independent\\n  practice, and closes with a retention check.\\n  \\n  A lesson teaches before it asks students to perform independently. It is not\\n  a worksheet, not a quick reference, and not a quiz. The teacher should be\\n  able to use it as the main classroom arc for first contact with a concept.\\n  \\n  The pedagogical arc is: orient -> build -> model -> practice -> close.\\n  Every section must serve that arc. The planner must not skip the modelling\\n  step, and it must not turn the close into a second teaching section.\\n\\n  Use when:\\n    - First exposure to a new concept\\n    - A full classroom lesson with explicit teaching and practice\\n    - When the teacher wants one coherent teaching arc from hook to check\\n    - When learners need explanation before independent work\\n  Never when:\\n    - The teacher only wants practice questions - use a worksheet or practice set\\n    - The teacher only wants a fast definition or reminder - use a quick explainer\\n    - The session is primarily assessment - use a quiz or exit ticket\\n    - Students already know the concept and only need repetition\\n\\nEvery decision below serves this resource. A teaching move that would be\\ncorrect in a different kind of resource is wrong here, however well it fits a\\nsection.\\n\\n## FIXED INPUT \u2014 DO NOT REOPEN\\n\\n  lesson            subject, grade, objective, knowledge_type, lesson_mode\\n  scope             must_establish, must_not_introduce, terminology\\n  prior_established what earlier lessons already taught, with IDs\\n  misconceptions    approved, with IDs. Zero to three. Never invent one.\\n  anchor            the concrete case, with ID. Chosen upstream.\\n  slots             ordered, each with a fixed purpose and typical_intents\\n  visual_required   boolean per slot; true is an authoritative visual contract\\n  required_visual_slots supplied slots whose visual contract must be fulfilled\\n  approved_item_ids assessment items already written and approved, with IDs\\n  assessment_source_policy\\n                    typed item IDs, eligible assessment intents, and the\\n                    exact ownership limits for source_question_ids\\n  limits            max blocks per section, max blocks per lesson\\n\\n## THE VOCABULARY\\n\\n  permitted intents   every intent you may use. Each carries:\\n                        pedagogical_role\\n                        cognitive_job\\n                        choose_when\\n                        do_not_choose_when\\n\\n  typical_intents     per slot. What that slot usually needs.\\n                      A starting point, NOT a fence.\\n\\n  excluded intents    with reasons. A hard wall. Never use one.\\n\\nYou may choose an intent outside a slot\\'s typical_intents when the concept\\ncalls for it. That is a normal, expected decision. It requires a\\ndeparture_reason and nothing more.\\n\\n## VISUAL REQUIREMENTS\\n\\nSome fixed slots carry `visual_required: true` because the objective is\\nspatial or describes a process. Treat that flag as a contract, not a\\nsuggestion. Every such slot must contain at least one teaching block whose\\nintent makes the visual job explicit: use `show-structure`, `trace-flow`,\\n`sequence`, or `name-parts` when one of those permitted intents\\nfits the objective. The block\\'s brief must state the exact subject, the\\nrequired labels or stages, the relationship the learner must notice, and how\\nthe anchor is used. For a process, name every required stage and direction in\\nthe brief; do not collapse a required sequence into a generic reference.\\n\\nUse the exact labels, stages, and relationships stated in `lesson.objective`\\nand `scope.must_establish`, together with the supplied `visual_required` and\\n`required_visual_slots` flags, as the source of truth. Do not invent labels,\\nstages, or relationships, and do not satisfy a required visual with prose\\nalone. Keep the decision pedagogical: never name a page\\nobject ID, component, layout, renderer, or other implementation detail in an\\nintent or brief. The writer will turn the approved teaching job into the\\nappropriate visual later.\\n\\nWhen `visual_required` is false, add a visual job only when it materially\\nimproves understanding and the slot\\'s permitted vocabulary supports it. A\\nvisual must never be decorative or a substitute for the explanation.\\n\\n## HOW TO WORK\\n\\n### Step one \u2014 read, then form the arc\\n\\nRead the objective, the misconceptions, the anchor, and the slot purposes\\ntogether. Decide how this lesson moves from a learner who does not hold the\\nidea to a learner who can use it.\\n\\nWrite that as `arc`: two or three sentences a teacher could read aloud.\\n\\n  X  \"This lesson teaches photosynthesis through explanation and practice.\"\\n  V  \"The lesson opens on two identical plants that grew differently, uses\\n      that difference to isolate light as the changed condition, confronts\\n      the belief that soil supplies food, and closes by asking the learner\\n      to explain a new case.\"\\n\\nThe arc is a commitment. Everything after it must serve it.\\n\\n### Step two \u2014 decide how the anchor is used\\n\\nThe anchor is fixed. You decide what each section does with it.\\n\\nIntroduce it once. Return to it. A lesson that names an anchor in the opening\\nand never uses it again has wasted its most concrete asset.\\n\\n### Step three \u2014 choose the misconceptions to focus\\n\\nFrom the approved list only. Zero is a legitimate answer for a recall-heavy\\nobjective. Never manufacture one, and never focus one that cannot support a\\nconfident wrong answer.\\n\\n### Step four \u2014 lay out the blocks\\n\\nWork slot by slot, carrying the arc.\\n\\nFor each slot:\\n\\n  1. Read the slot purpose against the objective and the anchor.\\n     What must happen HERE, for THIS concept?\\n\\n  2. Read choose_when for the typical intents.\\n     Then read do_not_choose_when.\\n\\n     do_not_choose_when is the important one. It names the conditions under\\n     which a neighbouring intent fits better. If a clause describes this\\n     concept, that intent is out.\\n\\n  3. If none of the typical intents fits, look at the permitted list.\\n     Choosing from it is normal. Write a departure_reason.\\n\\n  4. Decide how many blocks. One is common. Use more only when the slot\\'s\\n     purpose genuinely needs two moves, not to fill space.\\n\\n  5. Write the brief.\\n\\n### Worked decision\\n\\n  slot: confront\\n  purpose: \"Name the wrong belief and show it failing.\"\\n  typical_intents: [diagnose-misconception, warn, compare]\\n  objective: \"explain why plants cannot make food in darkness\"\\n  misconception-1: \"plants take food from the soil\"\\n\\n    diagnose-misconception\\n      choose_when: a named, real misconception exists and a wrong answer can\\n                   be written that only a learner holding it would confidently\\n                   choose\\n      -> misconception-1 is on the card and is exactly this. Matches.\\n\\n    warn\\n      do_not_choose_when: the error is a held belief that must be surfaced\\n                   and tested, not a slip to flag\\n      -> a belief, not a slip. Out.\\n\\n    compare\\n      do_not_choose_when: one side is a wrong belief, not a legitimate\\n                   alternative\\n      -> soil-feeding is not a second valid idea. Out.\\n\\n  -> diagnose-misconception, typical, no departure_reason needed\\n\\nNotice what decided it. Not the slot name and not the intent label. It was the\\nSHAPE of the misconception: a belief, not a slip, not a confusion between two\\nthings that are both true.\\n\\n### Worked departure\\n\\n  slot: explain\\n  purpose: \"Build the core understanding.\"\\n  typical_intents: [explain, explain-cause]\\n  objective: \"explain why plants cannot make food in darkness\"\\n\\n  Both typical intents are plausible. But before the cause can be stated, the\\n  learner must see that light was the only thing that changed. That is a\\n  controlled contrast, not an explanation.\\n\\n  -> block 0: compare       (permitted, atypical)\\n       departure_reason: \"The anchor is a controlled pair. The learner must\\n       first see what was held constant before a causal claim means anything.\\n       Explaining the cause first would leave the contrast unexamined.\"\\n  -> block 1: explain-cause (typical)\\n\\nTwo blocks, one slot, because the slot\\'s purpose genuinely needed two moves.\\n\\n## THE BRIEF\\n\\nThe brief is what the writer works from. Anything you leave out will not\\nappear on the page.\\n\\nA brief must answer:\\n\\n  What exact content belongs here?\\n  Which concept, case, or term does it use?\\n  What should change in the learner\\'s understanding?\\n  What must it NOT do?\\n\\n  X  \"Explain the cause clearly.\"\\n  X  \"Give a useful example.\"\\n  X  \"Introduce the topic in an engaging way.\"\\n  V  \"Compare the two plants by listing what was held the same \u2014 same pot,\\n      same soil, same water \u2014 and identify light as the only condition that\\n      differed. Do not yet say what light does.\"\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n  - Name concrete things. The anchor by name, the actual terms, the real\\n    numbers. A brief that says \"an example\" produces a generic example.\\n  - Use terminology from scope.terminology. Never use a term from\\n    must_not_introduce.\\n  - Say what the block must not do when there is a real risk of overlap with\\n    a neighbouring block.\\n  - If your brief would fit another lesson unchanged, it is too vague.\\n  - Never name a format. No prose, table, list, figure, worked example,\\n    questions, aside, choices. Describe what must be said, not what shape\\n    says it.\\n\\n**The last brief matters as much as the first.** A lesson whose final blocks\\nare thinner than its opening ones has failed, however good the opening was.\\nBefore you finish, re-read your last brief against your first.\\n\\n## EVIDENCE\\n\\nEvery block carries two evidence fields. Both are required.\\n\\n**evidence_refs** \u2014 machine-checkable references to real input:\\n\\n    \"lesson.objective\"\\n    \"scope.must_establish[0]\"\\n    \"misconception-1\"\\n    \"anchor.anchor-plant-window\"\\n    \"prior-1\"\\n    \"slot.explain.purpose\"\\n\\nOnly reference IDs that exist in the input. Every ref is validated.\\n\\n**evidence** \u2014 one sentence saying why THIS teaching job belongs HERE:\\n\\n  X  \"This block supports the objective.\"\\n  X  \"This seemed pedagogically appropriate.\"\\n  X  \"The slot needs an explanation.\"\\n  V  \"The objective asks WHY food is not made in darkness. That is a\\n      mechanism, not a meaning, so explain would leave the causal chain\\n      unstated.\"\\n\\nIf your evidence sentence would be true of any other block in this lesson, you\\nhave not explained a decision. Write it again.\\n\\n## APPROVED ASSESSMENT SOURCES\\n\\nYou may attach an already-approved item to a teaching block only under the\\nrules in `assessment_source_policy`.\\n\\nFor the current multiple-choice items, `source_question_ids` is either empty\\nor a singleton array containing exactly one approved item ID. Select the one\\nitem that best checks the lesson objective. You do not need to use every\\napproved item, and you must not collect several items into one block.\\n\\nA non-empty `source_question_ids` is legal only when the block\\'s intent is in\\n`assessment_source_policy.eligible_intents`. Leave it empty on every other\\nblock. Each approved item may be owned by at most one block.\\n\\nYou may NOT write question text, stems, options, or answers. Those already\\nexist and were approved separately. You also do not choose whether an item is\\nrendered as open response or multiple choice; its typed source record decides\\nthat later.\\n\\n## PROHIBITIONS\\n\\n  Never change the objective, scope, anchor identity, slot set, or slot order.\\n  Never use an excluded intent.\\n  Never invent a misconception, or focus one not on the approved list.\\n  Never name a page object, format, or layout anywhere in your output.\\n  Never emit a heading block. Headings come from section titles.\\n  Never write question content.\\n  Never put multiple approved item IDs in one multiple-choice source array.\\n  Never attach an approved item to a non-assessment intent.\\n  Never exceed the block limits.\\n  Never introduce a term from must_not_introduce.\\n\\n## OUTPUT\\n\\nJSON only. No preamble, no code fences, no commentary.\\n\\n{\\n  \"arc\": str,\\n  \"anchor_usage\": { \"<slot_id>\": str, ... },\\n  \"misconception_focus_ids\": [str],\\n  \"sections\": [\\n    {\\n      \"slot_id\": str,\\n      \"specific_purpose\": str,\\n      \"transition\": str | null,\\n      \"blocks\": [\\n        {\\n          \"id\": str,\\n          \"position\": int,\\n          \"intent\": str,\\n          \"brief\": str,\\n          \"evidence_refs\": [str],\\n          \"evidence\": str,\\n          \"departure_reason\": str | null,\\n          \"source_question_ids\": [str]\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\nBlock IDs follow `{slot_id}-b{n}`, starting at 1. Positions are 0-based and\\ncontiguous within each section. Sections appear in skeleton order.\\n\\n`transition` is null for the first section. For every other section, say in\\none sentence what the previous section left the learner ready for.\\n\\n## SELF-CHECK\\n\\n  1. Does my arc describe THIS lesson, or any lesson on this topic?\\n  2. Is my last brief as concrete as my first? Re-read both.\\n  3. Did I read do_not_choose_when, or recognise a label?\\n  4. Does every evidence sentence explain a choice, or restate the slot?\\n  5. Is the anchor introduced once and returned to?\\n  6. Does any brief name a format? Remove it.\\n  7. Would any two blocks teach the same thing?\\n  8. Does CHECK actually check the objective, or something adjacent?\\n  9. Is every focused misconception confronted somewhere?\\n 10. Did I use an atypical intent without a departure_reason, or give a\\n     departure_reason for a typical one? Both are errors.\\n 11. Is every non-empty source_question_ids a singleton on an eligible\\n     assessment intent, with no item reused by another block?\\n'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"$defs\": {\"AnchorUsage\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"orient\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"explain\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"confront\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"check\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"title\": \"AnchorUsage\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"TeachingPlanBlock\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"id\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"position\": {\"minimum\": 0, \"type\": \"integer\"}, \"intent\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"brief\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"evidence_refs\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"evidence\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"departure_reason\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}, \"source_question_ids\": {\"description\": \"Approved assessment-item ownership. Leave empty for a non-assessment block. A multiple-choice source must be the only ID in this array.\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"required\": [\"id\", \"position\", \"intent\", \"brief\", \"evidence\"], \"title\": \"TeachingPlanBlock\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"TeachingPlanSection\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"slot_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"specific_purpose\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"transition\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}, \"blocks\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/TeachingPlanBlock\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"required\": [\"slot_id\"], \"title\": \"TeachingPlanSection\", \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"arc\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"anchor_usage\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/AnchorUsage\"}, \"misconception_focus_ids\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"sections\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/TeachingPlanSection\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"required\": [\"arc\", \"anchor_usage\"], \"title\": \"TeachingPlan\", \"type\": \"object\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '{\\n  \"assessment_source_policy\": {\\n    \"allowed_evidence_refs\": [\\n      \"M1\",\\n      \"M2\",\\n      \"anchor.anchor-1\",\\n      \"item.5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i1\",\\n      \"item.5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i2\",\\n      \"item.5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i3\",\\n      \"item.5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i4\",\\n      \"item.5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i5\",\\n      \"lesson.objective\",\\n      \"must-1\",\\n      \"must-2\",\\n      \"must-3\",\\n      \"must-4\",\\n      \"prior-1\",\\n      \"prior-2\",\\n      \"prior-3\",\\n      \"prior-4\",\\n      \"prior-5\",\\n      \"prior-6\",\\n      \"prior-7\",\\n      \"prior-8\",\\n      \"prior-9\",\\n      \"scope.must_establish.must-1\",\\n      \"scope.must_establish.must-2\",\\n      \"scope.must_establish.must-3\",\\n      \"scope.must_establish.must-4\",\\n      \"scope.must_establish[0]\",\\n      \"scope.must_establish[1]\",\\n      \"scope.must_establish[2]\",\\n      \"scope.must_establish[3]\",\\n      \"slot.check.purpose\",\\n      \"slot.guided.purpose\",\\n      \"slot.model.purpose\",\\n      \"slot.orient.purpose\",\\n      \"slot.recall.purpose\"\\n    ],\\n    \"approved_sources\": [\\n      {\\n        \"approved_item_id\": \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i1\",\\n        \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"approved_item_id\": \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i2\",\\n        \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"approved_item_id\": \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i3\",\\n        \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"approved_item_id\": \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i4\",\\n        \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"approved_item_id\": \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i5\",\\n        \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"eligible_intents\": [\\n      \"activate-prior-knowledge\",\\n      \"apply\",\\n      \"check-understanding\",\\n      \"classify\",\\n      \"compare\",\\n      \"connect-back\",\\n      \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n      \"explain-cause\",\\n      \"interpret\",\\n      \"orient\",\\n      \"practise-guided\",\\n      \"practise-independent\",\\n      \"sequence\"\\n    ],\\n    \"forbidden_terminology\": [\\n      \"transpiration\",\\n      \"sublimation\",\\n      \"groundwater flow\",\\n      \"water table\",\\n      \"detailed cloud formation beyond condensation\",\\n      \"atmospheric layers\"\\n    ],\\n    \"rules\": {\\n      \"item_kind_is_fixed_upstream\": true,\\n      \"multiple_choice_ids_per_block\": \"0_or_1\",\\n      \"reuse_across_blocks\": \"forbidden\",\\n      \"selection_is_optional\": true,\\n      \"source_only_on_eligible_intent\": true\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \"fixed_input\": {\\n    \"anchor\": {\\n      \"description\": \"A puddle on a playground after a rainstorm, slowly disappearing over sunny days, then reappearing a\\\\u2026\",\\n      \"id\": \"anchor-1\"\\n    },\\n    \"approved_item_ids\": [\\n      \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i1\",\\n      \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i2\",\\n      \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i3\",\\n      \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i4\",\\n      \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i5\"\\n    ],\\n    \"lesson\": {\\n      \"grade_level\": \"Grade 6\",\\n      \"knowledge_type\": \"conceptual\",\\n      \"lesson_mode\": \"first_exposure\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Draw a clear labelled diagram of the water cycle showing evaporation, condensation, precipitation, collection, and the movement of water, and describe the cycle using the diagram\",\\n      \"path_lesson_id\": \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9\",\\n      \"subject\": \"Science\"\\n    },\\n    \"limits\": {\\n      \"max_blocks_per_section\": 3,\\n      \"max_sections\": 4,\\n      \"max_total_blocks\": 10\\n    },\\n    \"misconceptions\": [\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"M1\",\\n        \"statement\": \"{\\'id\\': \\'M1\\', \\'description\\': \\'Clouds are made of water vapor, so they should be labelled as gas.\\', \\'source\\': \\'drafted\\'}\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"M2\",\\n        \"statement\": \"{\\'id\\': \\'M2\\', \\'description\\': \\'The water cycle is a one-way path: water goes from the ocean to clouds to rain, and then stops.\\', \\'source\\': \\'drafted\\'}\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"prior_established\": [\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-1\",\\n        \"statement\": \"water exists in different states (solid, liquid, gas)\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-2\",\\n        \"statement\": \"water is found in different places such as rivers, lakes, oceans, clouds, and rain\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-3\",\\n        \"statement\": \"water can change state when heated or cooled\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-4\",\\n        \"statement\": \"define evaporation as the change from liquid water to water vapor caused by heat from the Sun\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-5\",\\n        \"statement\": \"define condensation as the change from water vapor to liquid water forming clouds\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-6\",\\n        \"statement\": \"define precipitation as water falling from clouds as rain, snow, sleet, or hail\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-7\",\\n        \"statement\": \"define collection as the gathering of water in rivers, lakes, and oceans\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-8\",\\n        \"statement\": \"explain that the Sun\\'s heat drives evaporation from bodies of water\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-9\",\\n        \"statement\": \"describe the sequence: evaporation \\\\u2192 condensation \\\\u2192 precipitation \\\\u2192 collection, linking each process to the next\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"required_visual_slots\": [\\n      \"model\"\\n    ],\\n    \"scope\": {\\n      \"must_establish\": [\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"must-1\",\\n          \"statement\": \"Define evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection in the context of the water cycle\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"must-2\",\\n          \"statement\": \"Explain how the Sun\\'s energy drives evaporation\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"must-3\",\\n          \"statement\": \"Describe the sequence of processes that move water through the cycle\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"must-4\",\\n          \"statement\": \"Construct a labelled diagram showing the water cycle\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \"must_not_introduce\": [\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"exclude-1\",\\n          \"statement\": \"transpiration\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"exclude-2\",\\n          \"statement\": \"sublimation\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"exclude-3\",\\n          \"statement\": \"groundwater flow\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"exclude-4\",\\n          \"statement\": \"water table\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"exclude-5\",\\n          \"statement\": \"detailed cloud formation beyond condensation\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"id\": \"exclude-6\",\\n          \"statement\": \"atmospheric layers\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \"terminology\": [\\n        \"evaporation\",\\n        \"condensation\",\\n        \"precipitation\",\\n        \"collection\",\\n        \"water cycle\",\\n        \"water vapor\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \"slots\": [\\n      {\\n        \"max_blocks\": 2,\\n        \"min_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"purpose\": \"Give the learner a reason to care and a concrete situation to hold.\",\\n        \"slot_id\": \"orient\",\\n        \"typical_intents\": [\\n          \"orient\",\\n          \"activate-prior-knowledge\",\\n          \"emphasise\"\\n        ],\\n        \"visual_required\": false\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"max_blocks\": 2,\\n        \"min_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"purpose\": \"Reactivate the specific prior knowledge this lesson depends on. Nothing else.\",\\n        \"slot_id\": \"recall\",\\n        \"typical_intents\": [\\n          \"activate-prior-knowledge\",\\n          \"define\",\\n          \"summarise\"\\n        ],\\n        \"visual_required\": false\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"max_blocks\": 3,\\n        \"min_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"purpose\": \"Show the method being carried out, with each step\\'s reasoning visible.\",\\n        \"slot_id\": \"model\",\\n        \"typical_intents\": [],\\n        \"visual_required\": true\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"max_blocks\": 3,\\n        \"min_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"purpose\": \"Practice with scaffolding still present. Learner does the thinking, support stays.\",\\n        \"slot_id\": \"guided\",\\n        \"typical_intents\": [],\\n        \"visual_required\": false\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"max_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"min_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"purpose\": \"The shared diagnostic. Identical across every variant. Written from the card only, never from the prose above it.\\\\n\",\\n        \"slot_id\": \"check\",\\n        \"typical_intents\": [\\n          \"check-understanding\",\\n          \"practise-independent\",\\n          \"diagnose-misconception\"\\n        ],\\n        \"visual_required\": false\\n      }\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \"legality_catalogue_hash\": \"707fa7c98e13d2a5e1f6b8fd5daadaa2c3b7851f51a6709bcc949d61ad27dd97\",\\n  \"repair\": {\\n    \"assessment_source_policy\": {\\n      \"allowed_evidence_refs\": [\\n        \"M1\",\\n        \"M2\",\\n        \"anchor.anchor-1\",\\n        \"item.5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i1\",\\n        \"item.5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i2\",\\n        \"item.5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i3\",\\n        \"item.5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i4\",\\n        \"item.5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i5\",\\n        \"lesson.objective\",\\n        \"must-1\",\\n        \"must-2\",\\n        \"must-3\",\\n        \"must-4\",\\n        \"prior-1\",\\n        \"prior-2\",\\n        \"prior-3\",\\n        \"prior-4\",\\n        \"prior-5\",\\n        \"prior-6\",\\n        \"prior-7\",\\n        \"prior-8\",\\n        \"prior-9\",\\n        \"scope.must_establish.must-1\",\\n        \"scope.must_establish.must-2\",\\n        \"scope.must_establish.must-3\",\\n        \"scope.must_establish.must-4\",\\n        \"scope.must_establish[0]\",\\n        \"scope.must_establish[1]\",\\n        \"scope.must_establish[2]\",\\n        \"scope.must_establish[3]\",\\n        \"slot.check.purpose\",\\n        \"slot.guided.purpose\",\\n        \"slot.model.purpose\",\\n        \"slot.orient.purpose\",\\n        \"slot.recall.purpose\"\\n      ],\\n      \"approved_sources\": [\\n        {\\n          \"approved_item_id\": \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i1\",\\n          \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"approved_item_id\": \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i2\",\\n          \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"approved_item_id\": \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i3\",\\n          \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"approved_item_id\": \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i4\",\\n          \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"approved_item_id\": \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i5\",\\n          \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \"eligible_intents\": [\\n        \"activate-prior-knowledge\",\\n        \"apply\",\\n        \"check-understanding\",\\n        \"classify\",\\n        \"compare\",\\n        \"connect-back\",\\n        \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n        \"explain-cause\",\\n        \"interpret\",\\n        \"orient\",\\n        \"practise-guided\",\\n        \"practise-independent\",\\n        \"sequence\"\\n      ],\\n      \"forbidden_terminology\": [\\n        \"transpiration\",\\n        \"sublimation\",\\n        \"groundwater flow\",\\n        \"water table\",\\n        \"detailed cloud formation beyond condensation\",\\n        \"atmospheric layers\"\\n      ],\\n      \"rules\": {\\n        \"item_kind_is_fixed_upstream\": true,\\n        \"multiple_choice_ids_per_block\": \"0_or_1\",\\n        \"reuse_across_blocks\": \"forbidden\",\\n        \"selection_is_optional\": true,\\n        \"source_only_on_eligible_intent\": true\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"instruction\": \"Return the complete corrected TeachingPlan JSON. Change only fields required to satisfy these errors. Use only intents listed under slot_intent_policy for each slot. For a multiple-choice assessment block, select zero or one approved item ID; never group IDs or reuse an ID. Attach it only to an assessment_source_policy eligible intent. Remove forbidden terminology and use only allowed_evidence_refs.\",\\n    \"legality_catalogue_hash\": \"707fa7c98e13d2a5e1f6b8fd5daadaa2c3b7851f51a6709bcc949d61ad27dd97\",\\n    \"previous_output\": {\\n      \"anchor_usage\": {\\n        \"check\": \"Not used.\",\\n        \"confront\": \"\",\\n        \"explain\": \"\",\\n        \"orient\": \"The puddle is introduced as the concrete phenomenon: a puddle on a sunny playground shrinks over days and reappears after rain, prompting the question of where the water goes and how it returns.\"\\n      },\\n      \"arc\": \"The lesson opens on a disappearing puddle that sparks curiosity about where water goes and how it returns. Students recall the meanings of evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection. The teacher models building a labelled diagram of the water cycle, emphasizing the circular flow and the distinction between water vapor and clouds. Guided practice has learners complete and describe their own diagrams, and the lesson closes with a multiple-choice check that diagnoses whether learners still think clouds are water vapor.\",\\n      \"misconception_focus_ids\": [\\n        \"M1\"\\n      ],\\n      \"sections\": [\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Present the anchor: a puddle on a playground after rain that slowly disappears over sunny days, then reappears after the next rain. Ask, \\'Where does the water go, and how does it return?\\' Do not explain the water cycle yet; only establish the phenomenon and the question.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": null,\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective requires students to draw and describe the water cycle. The anchor puddle provides a concrete, observable entry point that orients students to the phenomenon before any explanation, matching the orient slot purpose.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"lesson.objective\",\\n                \"anchor.anchor-1\",\\n                \"slot.orient.purpose\"\\n              ],\\n              \"id\": \"orient-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"orient\",\\n              \"position\": 0,\\n              \"source_question_ids\": []\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"orient\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Hook students with a familiar, puzzling observation to motivate the water cycle.\",\\n          \"transition\": null\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Prompt students to recall the definitions of evaporation (liquid to water vapor driven by Sun\\'s heat), condensation (water vapor to liquid forming clouds), precipitation (water falling as rain, snow, etc.), and collection (water gathering in rivers, lakes, oceans). Also have them state the sequence: evaporation \\\\u2192 condensation \\\\u2192 precipitation \\\\u2192 collection. Use quick verbal responses.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": null,\\n              \"evidence\": \"The lesson depends on prior knowledge of the water cycle processes defined in earlier lessons. Activating them here ensures students have the vocabulary and sequence ready before the model, fitting the recall slot\\\\u2019s purpose.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"prior-1\",\\n                \"prior-2\",\\n                \"prior-3\",\\n                \"prior-4\",\\n                \"prior-5\",\\n                \"prior-6\",\\n                \"prior-7\",\\n                \"prior-8\",\\n                \"prior-9\",\\n                \"slot.recall.purpose\"\\n              ],\\n              \"id\": \"recall-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"activate-prior-knowledge\",\\n              \"position\": 0,\\n              \"source_question_ids\": []\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"recall\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Reactivate the prior definitions and sequence of the water cycle processes needed for the diagram.\",\\n          \"transition\": \"The puddle observation raises questions about where water goes; now we recall the processes that explain it.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Draw a diagram step by step: (1) Place a large body of water at the bottom, label \\'Collection (ocean/lake)\\'. (2) Add the sun above, write \\'Sun\\'s energy\\'. (3) Draw an upward arrow from the water to the sky, label \\'Evaporation (water vapor)\\'. Explicitly note that water vapor is an invisible gas, so the arrow represents the vapor. (4) Draw cloud shapes at the top, label \\'Condensation (clouds)\\' and clarify that clouds are tiny liquid water droplets, not gas. (5) Draw a downward arrow from clouds to land and water, label \\'Precipitation (rain, etc.)\\'. (6) Add an arrow from land back to the ocean, label \\'Collection\\'. This block fulfills the visual requirement by showing labels and spatial arrangement.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": \"The slot has no typical intents; show-structure is chosen to explicitly build the spatial diagram and label parts, satisfying the visual_required contract.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective requires a labelled diagram showing evaporation, condensation, precipitation, collection, and the movement of water. The show-structure intent fulfills the visual requirement by constructing the spatial diagram with labels, making the arrangement explicit.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"lesson.objective\",\\n                \"scope.must_establish[0]\",\\n                \"scope.must_establish[3]\",\\n                \"slot.model.purpose\"\\n              ],\\n              \"id\": \"model-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"show-structure\",\\n              \"position\": 0,\\n              \"source_question_ids\": []\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Using the completed diagram, trace the flow: \\'The Sun\\'s energy causes evaporation from the ocean; water vapor rises and condenses into clouds (condensation); clouds release precipitation; water collects in the ocean again. The cycle repeats continuously \\\\u2014 it is not one-way.\\' Connect back to the puddle: puddle water evaporates, condenses into clouds, falls as rain, and re-fills the puddle. Emphasize the circular nature to pre-empt M2.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": \"The slot has no typical intents; sequence is chosen because the objective includes describing the ordered movement of water through the cycle, which is a natural fit for this intent.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective asks students to describe the cycle using the diagram, which demands understanding the order of processes. The sequence intent traces the flow of water through evaporation\\\\u2192condensation\\\\u2192precipitation\\\\u2192collection, the core sequence of the water cycle.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"lesson.objective\",\\n                \"scope.must_establish[2]\",\\n                \"slot.model.purpose\"\\n              ],\\n              \"id\": \"model-b2\",\\n              \"intent\": \"sequence\",\\n              \"position\": 1,\\n              \"source_question_ids\": []\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"model\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Demonstrate construction of a labelled water cycle diagram, revealing spatial arrangement and ordered flow.\",\\n          \"transition\": \"With the definitions fresh, we model how these processes form a continuous cycle in a diagram.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Provide a partially drawn diagram showing an ocean, sun, and cloud outlines, but missing the four process labels and arrows. Guide students to add the labels (evaporation, condensation, precipitation, collection) and draw the arrows showing water movement. Offer prompts: \\'What happens when the sun heats the water? Where does the water go after it evaporates? What are clouds made of?\\' Explicitly address M1: remind them that water vapor is an invisible gas, so clouds are not water vapor \\\\u2014 they are condensation (liquid water droplets). Walk around and give corrective feedback.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": \"The slot has no typical intents; practise-guided is the most direct way to have learners rehearse the diagram construction with teacher support, which is the central aim of this slot.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"After the model, students must attempt drawing the diagram themselves. Guided practice with a partially completed diagram provides support, fitting the slot\\'s purpose of \\'Practice with scaffolding still present\\' and directly targeting the construction of a labelled diagram (must_establish[3]).\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"scope.must_establish[3]\",\\n                \"M1\",\\n                \"slot.guided.purpose\"\\n              ],\\n              \"id\": \"guided-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"practise-guided\",\\n              \"position\": 0,\\n              \"source_question_ids\": []\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Ask students to turn to a partner and, using their completed diagram, describe the full water cycle, naming each process in order. Give sentence starters: \\'The Sun\\'s energy causes...\\', \\'When water vapor rises...\\', \\'After rain falls...\\'. Circulate and prompt for missing steps. This builds fluency in describing the cycle, addressing the second part of the objective.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": \"The slot has no typical intents; practise-guided is used again to scaffold the description task, ensuring students can articulate the cycle with prompts before independent work.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"Describing the cycle aloud partners with the diagram to meet the describing requirement of the objective. This second guided block moves from constructing the diagram to using it to explain, keeping support while shifting to verbal description.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"lesson.objective\",\\n                \"slot.guided.purpose\"\\n              ],\\n              \"id\": \"guided-b2\",\\n              \"intent\": \"practise-guided\",\\n              \"position\": 1,\\n              \"source_question_ids\": []\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"guided\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Allow learners to construct and describe the water cycle diagram with scaffolded support.\",\\n          \"transition\": \"After seeing the model, you will now draw and describe the cycle with support.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Administer a multiple-choice question that tests whether the learner correctly identifies that water vapor is an invisible gas and clouds are liquid water droplets. The item must have at least one distractor that labels clouds as water vapor (gas). Do not add new teaching; use only the approved item.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": null,\\n              \"evidence\": \"Misconception M1 (clouds are water vapor) directly contradicts correct understanding of condensation. This diagnostic checks whether the learner still holds that belief after instruction, fulfilling the check slot\\'s purpose as a shared diagnostic and targeting the misconception declared in the input.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"M1\",\\n                \"slot.check.purpose\",\\n                \"item.5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i1\"\\n              ],\\n              \"id\": \"check-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n              \"position\": 0,\\n              \"source_question_ids\": [\\n                \"5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:5222366a-8308-43e6-bb6c-4e4a0697f1d9:b959d3d6-87cb-48d7-9cb0-c113edbf2e0b.i1\"\\n              ]\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"check\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Diagnose whether students still confuse water vapor and clouds after instruction.\",\\n          \"transition\": \"Now you can show your understanding independently.\"\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \"slot_intent_policy\": {\\n      \"check\": {\\n        \"departure_rule\": \"A permitted non-typical intent requires a specific departure_reason. Excluded intents are never legal.\",\\n        \"permitted_departures\": [\\n          \"activate-prior-knowledge\",\\n          \"apply\",\\n          \"classify\",\\n          \"compare\",\\n          \"connect-back\",\\n          \"connect-forward\",\\n          \"define\",\\n          \"demonstrate\",\\n          \"derive\",\\n          \"emphasise\",\\n          \"explain\",\\n          \"explain-cause\",\\n          \"interpret\",\\n          \"memory-aid\",\\n          \"name-parts\",\\n          \"orient\",\\n          \"practise-guided\",\\n          \"sequence\",\\n          \"show-structure\",\\n          \"summarise\",\\n          \"trace-flow\",\\n          \"warn\"\\n        ],\\n        \"typical_intents\": [\\n          \"che